e quantum computer  so i'll tell you a little bit about our  center hopefully i won't take too much  of your time  okay so this all began uh uh back when  in the book of genesis when the national  quantum initiative uh dictated that the  national science foundation should  establish uh several quantum centers for  uh  for quantum information science research  and also for workforce development and  then uh that is then developed uh and uh  five of those centers now exist  so uh last year uh three of these  centers were founded uh ours which i'll  tell you about the q sense center which  is uh centered in uh in boulder  the h can center which is a centered in  the midwest in particular  university of illinois in  urbana-champaign and then uh recently in  the last couple of weeks  perhaps the last two we'll see how many  more there can be  quantum leap challenge institutes were  founded there's they don't really have  uh snazzy names yet but one of them is  uh centered at the university of  maryland focuses on quantum simulation  and the other one centered at the  university of chicago focuses on  quantum sensing for biological  applications whereas  the one in colorado is more in the time  and space division of sensing so you see  that among these quantum leap challenge  institutes ours is the one that is uh  dedicated specifically to uh to quantum  computing and so is maybe uh most  relevant for the gathering that we have  here uh today  so in founding the uh the ciqc we uh sat  together umesh was critical for these  discussions as were several other uh  colleagues and we asked ourselves you  know what's the special role that an  academic institute should now play in  this very new era of uh quantum  information science where there are  major industry efforts which many of you  represent there's major national lab  efforts which we'll hear about in a  couple of minutes  and other government efforts to try to  build a quantum computer with all these  um large entities working in this space  what's the right role for academic  institutes uh such as ours to play and  the focus has been on this concept of  future proofing which is really  a question of of time horizons you know  i think an academic institute ought to  look um you know far into the future you  know you pick your favorite time horizon  whether it's 10 or 20 or whatever number  of years and we should be uh considering  uh what what will be the obstacles that  uh stand in in the face of the future  developments that we hope to see  and then um  uh what what what obstacles uh will that  new development create um you know what  what will quantum computers uh wreak in  the world and and how can we overcome  those challenges as well  so uh thinking about that kind of time  horizon we thought was very  complementary to what is happening in in  industry industry will be  you know perhaps  more focused on on the nearer term on  specific technology uh choices and  particular approaches but uh but you  know can't also necessarily be as  concerned with with the far future also  that farther future is a place where  academia and industry can come together  and interact without you know too much  concern about competition and on  intellectual property and all you know  secrecy if we're we can together look at  the farther future and we all agree that  we should work together on addressing  problems that arise there  so the research effort is focused  focused on three specific challenges  which you'll recognize one is the  challenge of developing algorithms both  for the nisk but also the future era  that will make the effort to build  quantum computers worthwhile  the second is continuing this tradition  of trying to establish quantum advantage  over  classical uh information devices in a  wider range of problems of greater  relevance and also a wider range of  platforms and a third is tackling sort  of at a fundamental level what all of us  are facing which is that our quantum  