o participate in them as some  sort of mentors  we also hope that the ciqc can establish  sort of a  zone where academia and industry can get  together and interact and talk about  what are those  you know those coming challenges that we  can work on together  and then third of course we are building  up the quantum industry workforce and we  have some specific plans and you know i  can't talk about the real specifics but  um but we should have a robust program  for internships that would take place  over the summer for undergraduate  students we have a lot of undergrads  deeply interested in these and also  graduate students in both theory and  experiment and also our post-docs  so now with all the industrialists  sitting in the audience here's what i  want from you  one is uh i would love for you to  participate in this quantum research in  industrial projects for scientist  programs so here this is led out of ipam  at ucla it selects top-notch  undergraduates from around the country  to work with industry and academic  mentors on problems that are posed by  industry so we had a pilot project or  the first year project where  a question was posed by ibm dmitry  masloff acted as the  industry mentor and it was a tremendous  success uh four students worked with him  uh and uh produced a very impressive  report which is actually going to appear  in a publication hopefully soon so i  would love for you to contact us and  participate in this program and suggest  good questions to work on over the  summer with  undergraduates who would be living in  ucla during that time  a second is i there's a huge appetite  for uh summer internships to go work in  industry so  contact us let us know what would be  opportunities we'll do our job of  matching those ideas to the people that  we can provide from our center  a third is we'd love for you to join us  in sort of an industry consortium we're  forming two hubs that would either be  focused in the bay area or in  the los angele